EXPERIMENT, NOT APPLIED: charge an I/O-capped TB like the TB it replaced

Applies on top of patches/qemu/0017-io-split-cap.patch (reverted on `linux`;
recover it with `git show ee1d1e8:patches/qemu/0017-io-split-cap.patch`).

0017 caps a TB at its MMIO access and therefore counts the guest-instruction
budget exactly; stock QEMU's split path charged the whole original TB, then
ran the access in an uncounted CF_NOIRQ TB, then charged the suffix. The
interleave was calibrated under the stock charging. This records the uncapped
length per TB pc and charges a capped TB that much (ot_insn_charge_min).

Result on tests/trig8-repro.sh (3 runs): the deterministic every-8th drop is
gone, but drops remain SPORADIC ([2], [48, 77], []) where the build without
0017 is clean on every run (7+). So this does not reproduce stock accounting
exactly; see docs/PERF-NOTES.md.

